{"kind":"request","text":"Retire the comma-joined artifact paths and re-anchor the documentation resting on them, because twenty of the forty-six anchored pages currently cannot flare.\n\nAGENTS.md already names this defect and says it takes its own request. This is that request. It is the same shape as the whole-repository dot-artifact migration and should be run the same way.\n\nMeasured against the live log today, not inferred. Twenty-five live artifacts sit at comma-joined pseudo-paths. Paths match as exact strings, so a merge that changes internal/workroom/fold.go publishes at internal/workroom or at the file, never at the six-way string internal/app/app.go,internal/workroom/fold.go,internal/workroom/schema.go,internal/service/server.go,internal/intent/intent.go,internal/nexus/nexus.go. No successor can ever equal that string, so those artifacts are unretirable by any ordinary merge and everything anchored to them is permanently silent.\n\nTwenty of the forty-six documentation pages carrying rests_on cite at least one of them. The worst is docs/reference/gs/batch.md, whose single basis is a comma path, so that page can never flare at all, whatever happens to gs batch. Others are partly protected because they also cite a real path: docs/reference/architecture.md cites one comma path and one real one, so it flares on kernel changes and not on application ones.\n\nThis is not a criticism of any one head. It is the prevailing convention in this repository — I found it while reviewing an architecture page that followed it, and I did not block that head for doing what everything around it does. That is precisely why it needs a migration rather than case-by-case correction: reviewers cannot reasonably refuse the house style one page at a time, so the style has to change once, deliberately, with the anchors repaired in the same pass.\n\nTwo cautions from the dot migration, which cost more than expected. Retiring these propagates staleness into everything resting on them, so it should run when no review is waiting on a first verdict and no approved head is out of main. And most of the resulting flares will be history — finished acts whose bases moved afterwards — which is honest and needs nothing; the work is the live documentation, not the flare count.","body":{"conditions":"Every live artifact whose path field contains a comma is retired by a bare supersede naming the merge or reason that retires it, since no successor string can equal a comma path; where the area still needs an anchor, a first artifact is published at each real path the comma string named, at the commit the behaviour now lives at; every documentation page whose rests_on cited a retired comma artifact is re-anchored onto live artifacts at real paths, with docs/reference/gs/batch.md specifically checked because its only basis is a comma path; the prose of each re-anchored page is re-checked against what main now carries rather than only its frontmatter edited; a gate or check prevents a new artifact being recorded at a path containing a comma, so the convention cannot return; the migration runs when no review request is awaiting its first verdict and no approved head is out of main; the closing report states both the number of comma artifacts retired and the number of live artifacts still reaching one, and proves the first is zero; make test and go vet pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}